**user**:
> What is standard practice for relating 1-in-100 year events from a distribution of simulated events? Do I take the 99th percentile of the distribution?
**assistant**:
> Yes, taking the 99th percentile of your distribution of simulated events is the standard practice for identifying a 1-in-100 year event, also known as a centennial event. This percentile essentially represents the value below which 99% of your simulation outcomes fall, making the events at this threshold or above rare, occurring, on average, once in 100 years if the conditions remain consistent.
> 
> Keep in mind that the reliability of this estimate depends on the robustness of your model, the number of simulations you have run, and how well your distribution captures the relevant phenomena of interest. If you're interested in the tail behavior, you might also want to consider using extreme value theory, which can provide a more nuanced understanding of low-probability events by focusing on the asymptotic distribution of the extremes.
> 
> If you have further questions about interpreting these results or incorporating them into broader risk assessments, feel free to ask!
